schema_version: 1

# Draft curator-facing model catalog input.
#
# Purpose:
# - Keep this file human-writable.
# - Fill in only the labels you can actually read from the CLI or UI.
# - Do not translate values into runtime/internal forms.
#
# Runtime normalization is intentionally a later cats-runtime responsibility.
# Inheritance rules:
# - model with no `options` key inherits shared_options in scope
# - use `options: []` to say a model has no options
# - provider shared_options override same-named catalog shared_options
#
# Provenance is per-catalog and no longer uniform - read each entry's `notes`.
# Every catalog carries a `version` and `last_updated`; `last_updated` records
# when the model list itself was last re-read or otherwise verified. A newer CLI
# version probe alone does not advance that date; its observation date and scope
# belong in `notes` until the model list can also be re-verified.
#
# Seed data was originally backfilled from local CLI versions observed on
# 2026-04-08 plus the then-current cats-runtime static/advanced baselines.
#
# 2026-08-26 refresh: Antigravity, Codex, and Cursor are account-resolved live
# CLI enumerations (`agy models`, `codex debug models`, `cursor-agent models`).
# 2026-09-02: the Claude and Codex entries were re-read from their interactive
# `/model` pickers and now carry mixed provenance - see each entry's `notes`.
# 2026-09-03: the Antigravity entry was refreshed from `agy models` on 1.1.24
# corroborated by that build's `/model` picker, and a Grok entry was added for
# the first time from the Grok CLI 1.0.13 account-resolved model manifest.
# Every other entry keeps the provenance and date recorded in its own `notes`.
# Copilot, Kilo, Kiro, and Junie had their versions re-probed but not their model
# lists - those CLIs either gate the list behind an account, expose it only in an
# interactive picker, or publish a different artifact than the picker shows.
#
# Treat this file as a starting point to correct, trim, or expand.

catalogs:
  - cli: Claude
    version: 2.1.257
    last_updated: 2026-09-02
    notes:
      - "Model list re-read 2026-09-02 from the operator-pasted interactive `/model` picker on Claude Code 2.1.257; the operator confirmed all five visible rows were present with nothing scrolled off"
      - "Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/claude-2.1.257/model-picker.success.redacted.txt"
      - "The picker rows were Default (recommended), Opus (1M context), Fable, Sonnet, and Haiku, in that order. `Default` is a provider-default null sentinel and is retained in evidence rather than projected into a model id that the runtime would pass through `--model`. The `√` marker is recorded only as the current picker selection and is not interpreted as an account-default claim."
      - "Picker descriptions identify Opus 5, Fable 5.1, Sonnet 5, and Haiku 4.5. The `opus`, `sonnet`, and `haiku` aliases remain grounded in the earlier 2.1.246 static extraction; read-only `--help` and static extraction from the locally installed 2.1.258 executable independently expose `fable` and `claude-fable-5-1`. That newer artifact is raw-token evidence only, not account-entitlement evidence for 2.1.257."
      - "For Default, Opus, Fable, and Sonnet, the operator observed the complete ordered effort set Low, Medium, High, xHigh, Max, Ultracode, with High marked `(default)`. Haiku instead displayed `Effort not supported for Haiku`. The Default sentinel's effort branch remains evidence-only; the other three branches are represented per model."
      - "The locally installed 2.1.258 artifact maps the picker-visible Ultracode choice to the raw `ultracode` session value and describes it as xHigh effort plus dynamic workflow orchestration. A read-only `claude --effort ultracode --version` parser check accepted that token and returned 2.1.258; the top-level `--help` list itself still stops at Max."
      - "Opus 1M context was re-observed in the picker. Sonnet 1M and Haiku 200K context values are retained from the 2.1.246 static extraction and were not re-observed; Fable context is omitted because the 2.1.257 picker row did not state one."
      - "`default: true` on Opus is retained from the account-resolved 2026-04-17 snapshot. The 2.1.257 `√` marker was on the Default sentinel, so this refresh neither re-verifies nor removes that older explicit-alias default."
    models:
      - name: Opus
        label: Opus 5 (1M context)
        notes:
          - "Picker description: Opus 5 with 1M context - best for everyday, complex tasks"
        default: true
        context: 1000000
        options:
          - name: Effort
            values:
              - name: Low
                notes:
                  - "Quick, straightforward implementation with minimal overhead"
              - name: Medium
                notes:
                  - "Balanced approach with standard implementation and testing"
              - name: High
                notes:
                  - "Comprehensive implementation with extensive testing and documentation"
              - name: xHigh
                notes:
                  - "Deeper reasoning than high, just below maximum (Fable 5, Opus 4.7+, Sonnet 5)"
              - name: Max
                notes:
                  - "Maximum capability with deepest reasoning. May use excessive tokens resulting in long response times or overthinking. Use sparingly for the hardest tasks."
              - name: Ultracode
                notes:
                  - "2.1.258 static artifact description: xHigh effort plus dynamic workflow orchestration for this session only"
            default: High

      - name: Fable
        label: Fable 5.1
        notes:
          - "Picker description: Fable 5.1 - most capable for your hardest and longest-running tasks"
        options:
          - name: Effort
            values:
              - Low
              - Medium
              - High
              - xHigh
              - name: Max
                notes:
                  - "May use excessive tokens resulting in long response times or overthinking. Use sparingly for the hardest tasks."
              - name: Ultracode
                notes:
                  - "2.1.258 static artifact description: xHigh effort plus dynamic workflow orchestration for this session only"
            default: High

      - name: Sonnet
        label: Sonnet 5
        notes:
          - "Picker description: Sonnet 5 - efficient for routine tasks"
        context: 1000000
        options:
          - name: Effort
            values:
              - Low
              - Medium
              - High
              - xHigh
              - name: Max
                notes:
                  - "May use excessive tokens resulting in long response times or overthinking. Use sparingly for the hardest tasks."
              - name: Ultracode
                notes:
                  - "2.1.258 static artifact description: xHigh effort plus dynamic workflow orchestration for this session only"
            default: High

      - name: Haiku
        label: Haiku 4.5
        notes:
          - "Picker description: Haiku 4.5 - fastest for quick answers"
        context: 200000
        options: []

  - cli: Codex
    version: 0.152.0
    last_updated: 2026-09-02
    notes:
      - "Model list re-read 2026-09-02 from the authenticated interactive `/model` picker on Codex CLI 0.152.0; the operator confirmed all seven rows were visible with nothing scrolled off"
      - "Every model id, ordering, description, reasoning-level value set, and per-model reasoning default below was re-verified against that picker"
      - "Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/codex-0.152.0/model-picker.success.redacted.txt"
      - "The picker shows raw ids only. Every `label` and every `context` number still comes from the 2026-08-26 `codex debug models` run on 0.149.1 and was not re-observed here"
      - "`default: true` on gpt-5.6-sol likewise remains from that 0.149.1 `codex debug models` run. The 0.152.0 model list marks gpt-5.6-sol only `(current)`, which is the session selection; the reasoning screens print a separate `(default)` marker, so the model list itself makes no account-default claim either way"
      - "Only models with `visibility: list` are included; hidden internal models are omitted. That filter is from the 0.149.1 `codex debug models` output and was not re-checked"
      - "Context values reflect the Codex CLI catalog and may differ from OpenAI API limits"
      - "Max and Ultra sit behind a `More reasoning…` entry that opens an `Advanced Reasoning` screen headed `⚠ Consumes usage limits faster`. That nesting has no representation in this schema and is flattened into the value lists below. The picker gates it per model: gpt-5.6-sol and gpt-5.6-terra offer `Max and Ultra`, gpt-5.6-luna offers `Max` only, and gpt-5.5, gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4-mini, and gpt-5.3-codex-spark show no such entry at all"
      - "All three `More reasoning…` submenus were opened on 0.152.0 - gpt-5.6-sol, gpt-5.6-terra, and gpt-5.6-luna - so the per-model gating above is directly observed rather than read from the picker's hint text. gpt-5.5, gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4-mini, and gpt-5.3-codex-spark expose no `More reasoning…` entry to open"
      - "Max and Ultra descriptions are the 0.152.0 picker text and sit in shared_options on observed grounds: the Max line is byte-identical across all three models that offer it (gpt-5.6-sol, gpt-5.6-terra, gpt-5.6-luna) and the Ultra line is byte-identical across both that offer it (gpt-5.6-sol, gpt-5.6-terra). That matches Low, Medium, High, and Extra high, which render identically under all seven models, so Codex draws level descriptions from one table rather than per model. The wording they replace came from the runtime advanced-knowledge baseline and was never observed in any picker"
    shared_options:
      - name: Reasoning Level
        values:
          - name: Low
            notes:
              - "Fast responses with lighter reasoning"
          - name: Medium
            notes:
              - "Balances speed and reasoning depth for everyday tasks"
          - name: High
            notes:
              - "Greater reasoning depth for complex problems"
          - name: Extra high
            notes:
              - "Extra high reasoning depth for complex problems"
          - name: Max
            notes:
              - "For difficult problems when quality matters more than speed · higher usage"
          - name: Ultra
            notes:
              - "For demanding work using multiple agents · highest usage"
        default: Medium
    models:
      - name: gpt-5.6-sol
        label: GPT-5.6-Sol
        notes:
          - "Latest frontier agentic coding model."
        default: true
        context: 272000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            default: Low

      - name: gpt-5.6-terra
        label: GPT-5.6-Terra
        notes:
          - "Balanced agentic coding model for everyday work."
        context: 272000

      - name: gpt-5.6-luna
        label: GPT-5.6-Luna
        notes:
          - "Fast and affordable agentic coding model."
        context: 272000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            values: [Low, Medium, High, Extra high, Max]
            default: Medium

      - name: gpt-5.5
        label: GPT-5.5
        notes:
          - "Frontier model for complex coding, research, and real-world work."
        context: 272000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            values: [Low, Medium, High, Extra high]
            default: Medium

      - name: gpt-5.4
        label: GPT-5.4
        notes:
          - "Strong model for everyday coding."
        context: 272000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            values: [Low, Medium, High, Extra high]
            default: Medium

      - name: gpt-5.4-mini
        label: GPT-5.4-Mini
        notes:
          - "Small, fast, and cost-efficient model for simpler coding tasks."
        context: 272000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            values: [Low, Medium, High, Extra high]
            default: Medium

      - name: gpt-5.3-codex-spark
        label: GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark
        notes:
          - "Ultra-fast coding model."
        context: 128000
        options:
          - name: Reasoning Level
            values: [Low, Medium, High, Extra high]
            default: High

  - cli: Antigravity
    version: 1.1.24
    last_updated: 2026-09-03
    notes:
      - "Model list re-read 2026-09-03 from live `agy models` on 1.1.24, one `<id><tab><label>` pair per line; the command printed the whole list and exited 0, so the enumeration is complete for this account."
      - "Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/antigravity-1.1.24/models-command.success.redacted.txt"
      - "Independently corroborated the same day by the operator-pasted interactive `/model` picker on 1.1.24, which listed exactly the seven families these ids flatten to. Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/antigravity-1.1.24/model-picker.success.redacted.txt"
      - "`name` is the raw id `--model` accepts. A rejected id makes agy echo the labels back instead, so ids and labels are not interchangeable on input."
      - "The picker lists a model family plus a separate Effort slider, while `agy models` flattens the same choices into one id per effort level. Only the flat ids are represented here, because the ids are what the runtime passes through `--model`; the picker's family/Effort hierarchy is retained in the fixture rather than projected into curated `options`, which the Antigravity overlay does not consume."
      - "Effort coverage observed in the 1.1.24 picker: Gemini 3.8/3.7/3.6 Flash each expose low, medium, and high; Gemini 3.1 Pro exposes only low and high; the Claude and GPT-OSS rows cannot be adjusted at all. That matches the id set exactly."
      - "The per-level descriptions on the Flash rows below were read off the 1.1.24 Effort slider. Gemini 3.1 Pro's low/high descriptions were not captured and are deliberately not copied over from the Flash rows."
      - "Superseded the 1.1.20 listing: `gemini-3.5-flash-high`, `-medium`, and `-low` are absent from both 1.1.24 sources and were removed; `gemini-3.8-flash-*` is new."
      - "No entry is marked default: agy takes its default from the per-user settings.json `model` field, and the runtime omits `--model` entirely when nothing is selected, so naming one here would override a preference the runtime cannot see. The picker's `> ... (current)` marker on Gemini 3.8 Flash is recorded as the operator's current selection only, not as an account default."
    models:
      - name: gemini-3.8-flash-high
        label: Gemini 3.8 Flash (High)
        notes:
          - "Effort high: deepest reasoning for complex problems - slower but strongest"
      - name: gemini-3.8-flash-medium
        label: Gemini 3.8 Flash (Medium)
        notes:
          - "Effort medium: balanced speed and reasoning quality for most tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.8-flash-low
        label: Gemini 3.8 Flash (Low)
        notes:
          - "Effort low: faster responses, lighter reasoning - great for simpler tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.7-flash-high
        label: Gemini 3.7 Flash (High)
        notes:
          - "Effort high: deepest reasoning for complex problems - slower but strongest"
      - name: gemini-3.7-flash-medium
        label: Gemini 3.7 Flash (Medium)
        notes:
          - "Effort medium: balanced speed and reasoning quality for most tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.7-flash-low
        label: Gemini 3.7 Flash (Low)
        notes:
          - "Effort low: faster responses, lighter reasoning - great for simpler tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.6-flash-high
        label: Gemini 3.6 Flash (High)
        notes:
          - "Effort high: deepest reasoning for complex problems - slower but strongest"
      - name: gemini-3.6-flash-medium
        label: Gemini 3.6 Flash (Medium)
        notes:
          - "Effort medium: balanced speed and reasoning quality for most tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.6-flash-low
        label: Gemini 3.6 Flash (Low)
        notes:
          - "Effort low: faster responses, lighter reasoning - great for simpler tasks"
      - name: gemini-3.1-pro-high
        label: Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)
      - name: gemini-3.1-pro-low
        label: Gemini 3.1 Pro (Low)
      - name: claude-sonnet-4-6
        label: Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Thinking)
      - name: claude-opus-4-6-thinking
        label: Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking)
      - name: gpt-oss-120b-medium
        label: GPT-OSS 120B (Medium)

  - cli: Grok
    version: 1.0.13
    last_updated: 2026-09-03
    notes:
      - "First curated Grok entry; before 2026-09-03 the runtime carried only the 1.0.0 static fallback row `grok-4.5`."
      - "Model list read 2026-09-03 from the CLI's own account-resolved manifest at ~/.grok/models_cache.json, fetched 2026-09-02T17:48:31Z from https://cli-chat-proxy.grok.com/v1/models by Grok Build 1.0.13 with session auth. The manifest holds exactly these two models, so the list is complete for this account."
      - "Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/grok-1.0.13/models-cache.success.redacted.json"
      - "Corroborated the same day by `grok models` on 1.0.13, which printed both ids and exited 0, and by the operator-pasted interactive `/model` picker. Evidence: docs/research/fixtures/grok-1.0.13/models-command.success.redacted.txt and docs/research/fixtures/grok-1.0.13/model-picker.success.redacted.txt"
      - "`name` is the raw id `grok --model` accepts, taken from the manifest `id` field; `label` is the manifest `name` field, which is also what the picker renders."
      - "No entry is marked default. The manifest carries no default field at all; `grok models` prints `Default model: grok-4.6` and a `(default)` marker, but ~/.grok/config.toml on the observed machine sets `[models] default = \"grok-4.6\"`, so that marker reports a per-user preference rather than an account or vendor default. The adapter already omits `--model` when nothing is selected, so claiming one here would override a preference the runtime cannot see. This supersedes the older `grok-4.5 default: true` claim, which came from the 1.0.0 probe reading the same config-derived marker."
      - "Both models advertise `supports_reasoning_effort: true` and a per-model `reasoning_efforts` menu. Option value names below are the raw tokens `grok --reasoning-effort` (alias `--effort`) accepts, taken from the manifest `value` field; each value's note carries the menu label and description verbatim. The menus differ per model and are never shared: grok-4.6 offers four levels including `xhigh`, grok-4.5 only three."
      - "Effort menus were re-captured 2026-09-03 as per-model picker screens that each show the `/model <name>` prompt line, and they agree line for line with the manifest. Bundled `~/.grok/README.md` documents the wider flag vocabulary `none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max` plus per-model menu ids; only the account-resolved per-model menus are curated here."
      - "`default: high` on both models is the manifest's own `default: true` entry, not a picker marker. The observed machine's `~/.grok/config.toml` sets `default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`, which is why the picker showed Extra High as `(active)` for grok-4.6; that per-user override is deliberately not encoded here."
      - "`context: 500000` for both models is the manifest `context_window` field. `max_completion_tokens` is null upstream and is therefore omitted rather than guessed."
    models:
      - name: grok-4.6
        label: Grok 4.6
        notes:
          - "Manifest description: SpaceXAI's latest frontier model"
        context: 500000
        options:
          - name: Effort
            values:
              - name: xhigh
                notes:
                  - "Extra High Effort - Highest effort and reasoning level"
              - name: high
                notes:
                  - "High Effort - Higher implementation quality with extensive reasoning"
              - name: medium
                notes:
                  - "Medium Effort - Balanced effort with standard implementation and testing"
              - name: low
                notes:
                  - "Low Effort - Quick, fast implementations"
            default: high
      - name: grok-4.5
        label: Grok 4.5
        notes:
          - "The manifest carries no description for this model; the picker row shows the label alone."
        context: 500000
        options:
          - name: Effort
            values:
              - name: high
                notes:
                  - "High Effort - Highest implementation quality with extensive reasoning"
              - name: medium
                notes:
                  - "Medium Effort - Balanced effort with standard implementation and testing"
              - name: low
                notes:
                  - "Low Effort - Quick, fast implementations"
            default: high

